  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Disaster Resilience of at-risk communities in Timor-Leste (STREAM)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2020, the legal basis and organic law for civil protection in Timor-Leste were approved. The law mandated Civil Protection Authority (CPA) as the government agency responsible for disaster risk reduction (DRR) and disaster risk management (DRM). As a recently established authority, CPA&apos;s policies and guidelines, such as national DRM strategy, contingency plans, and standard operating procedures (SOPs), have yet to be established. At the same time, CPA is pushing for the de-centralization of its mandate at the subnational level. Regional and village civil protection councils (MCPCs and SCPCs) are expected to prepare and respond to disasters to the best of their capacities before the national government intervenes. In response, IOM Timor Leste proposes a two-pronged approach, national government technical assistance and community empowerment, to strengthen at-risk populations&apos; resilience to disasters brought about by rapid and slow-onset hazards. The project aims to (1) improve the functional capacity of CPA to prepare for and respond to emergencies through inclusive and gender-responsive programs on DRM and, at the same time, (2) influence the reduction of disaster risk in at-risk communities, especially among women, girls, older persons, and persons with disabilities through inclusive participation and improved access to evacuation centers.</narrative>
  </description>
